L000002	\title{{\bf Supplementary Material for the article:}\\ ``Tunable lower critical fractal dimension for a non-equilibrium phase transition"}
L000003	\setcounter{page}{1}
L000004	\setcounter{equation}{0}
L000005	\setcounter{figure}{0}
L000006	\renewcommand{\theequation}{S\arabic{equation}}
L000007	\renewcommand{\thefigure}{S\arabic{figure}}
L000008	
L000009	\date{\today}
L000010	\maketitle
L000011	
L000012	\onecolumngrid
L000013	
L000014	This Supplementary Material provides further theoretical details and additional numerical results that support and extend the findings presented in the main text.
L000015	
L000016	\section{Numerical method}
L000017	
L000018	We numerically simulate the driven--dissipative Gross--Pitaevskii equation [Eq.~(\ref{eq:GPE})] on a two-dimensional spatial grid. The system is initialized in the vacuum state, $\psi(\mathbf{r},t=0)=0$ for all $\mathbf{r}$. At time $t=0$, the coherent pump is switched on with a fixed driving amplitude $F$, corresponding to a driving intensity $I = F^2$. The equation is then time evolved up to a final time $t_f$, chosen sufficiently large for the dynamics to converge to the steady state. Time integration is performed using a second-order Runge--Kutta scheme with a fixed time step selected to ensure numerical stability.
L000019	
L000020	To characterize the long-time dynamics, we compute the spatially averaged density $\bar{n}(t)=\int \mathrm{d}^2x\, n(\mathbf{r},t)$ and apply a Fourier transform to its time evolution. From the resulting spectrum, we extract the asymptotic decay rate $\lambda$ by measuring the full width at half maximum (FWHM) of the central peak, which provides the slowest relaxation rate of the system.
L000021	
L000022	We have verified the robustness of our results by including quantum fluctuations within the Truncated Wigner Approximation, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:TWA}. The driven-dissipative Gross--Pitaevskii equation is found to capture the  behavior remarkably well, as the spatial inhomogeneities induced by the driving pattern effectively act as \textit{spatial fluctuations}~\cite{Li2022}.
L000023	\begin{figure}
L000024	    \centering
L000025	    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures_SM/TWAvsGPE.pdf}
L000026	    \caption{First-order phase transition observed within the Truncated Wigner Approximation. Here, we consider the same 1D system as in the main text, with a longitudinal size $L=34.5\xi$ and $\Delta=3\gamma$. The observables are averaged over 100 trajectories.}
L000027	    \label{fig:TWA}
L000028	\end{figure}
L000029	
L000030	
L000031	\section{The effect of detuning for a Sierpinski carpet}
L000032	
L000033	In the main text, we characterize critical behavior in terms of the minimal asymptotic decay rate. Here, we further examine how the asymptotic decay rate depends on the driving intensity for different system sizes. In Fig.~\ref{fig:SC-PT markers}(a)--(b), these quantities are shown for the Sierpinski carpet (second column in Fig.~\ref{fig:patterns-driving+densities}) with $\Delta=\gamma$, a regime where no phase transition occurs. The minimal asymptotic decay rate exhibits a clear minimum around a critical driving intensity $I\approx2.2\gamma^2$, yet this minimum saturates to a finite value.
L000034	
L000035	
L000036	\begin{figure}[t]
L000037	    \centering
L000038	    \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures_SM/carpet_ADR+n_Delta1+2.pdf}
L000039	
L000040	
L000041	    \caption{Response of the system to a Sierpinski carpet drive with fractal dimension $d_H = 1.84$.
L000042	(a) Asymptotic decay rate as a function of input intensity for detuning $\Delta = \gamma$, showing no phase transition.
L000043	(b) Corresponding bosonic density as a function of input intensity, with a smooth increase.
L000044	(c) For a larger detuning $\Delta = 2.0\gamma$, the minimal asymptotic decay rate $\lambda_{\min}$ vanishes at the critical point $I_c \approx 7.1\gamma^2$, indicating a first-order phase transition.
L000045	(d) At the same critical point, the average bosonic density exhibits a discontinuous jump.}
L000046	
L000047	    \label{fig:SC-PT markers}
L000048	\end{figure}
L000049	
L000050	In Fig.~\ref{fig:SC-PT markers}(c)--(d), we plot the same quantities for a larger detuning, $\Delta=2\gamma$. In this case, at a higher driving intensity $I_c\approx7.1\gamma^2$, the minimal asymptotic decay rate $\lambda_{\min}$ collapses to numerical precision, and the density exhibits an abrupt switch from the lower to the upper branch of the bistability. These features indicate the occurrence of a first-order phase transition.
L000051	
L000052	
L000053	\section{Fractal dimension : nominal dimension vs box-counting dimension}
L000054	
L000055	In this section, we discuss different notions of fractal dimension and clarify the distinction between nominal and box-counting definitions. The nominal mathematical fractal dimension \( d \) of a Sierpinski-carpet-like object can be determined from its self-similar scaling properties. If the pattern is constructed by dividing a square into \( s \times s \) smaller squares and removing a given number of them (the “holes”), then at each iteration the remaining area scales as
L000056	\[
L000057	A = s^2 - N_{\text{holes}}.
L000058	\]
L000059	This area corresponds to the white surface shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:dimension schema}. The nominal fractal dimension is then obtained from the ratio of logarithms,
L000060	\[
L000061	d^{th}_H = \frac{\log(A)}{\log(s)} = \frac{\log(s^2 - N_{\text{holes}})}{\log(s)}.
L000062	\]
L000063	This expression characterizes how the number of occupied sites, or equivalently the total area, scales with the rescaling factor \( s \) for a \textit{theoretical} infinitely self-similar fractal. The procedure is illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:dimension schema} for the specific case of the Sierpinski carpet.
L000064	
L000065	
L000066	\begin{figure}[h]
L000067	\centering
L000068	\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.95]
L000069	\begin{scope}
L000070	    \draw (0,0) rectangle (3,3);
L000071	
L000072	    \node at (1.5,-0.5) {simple square} ;
L000073	\end{scope}
L000074	
L000075	\begin{scope}[shift={(5,0)}]
L000076	    \draw (0,0) rectangle (3,3);
L000077	    \draw [fill=black] (1,1) rectangle (2,2) ;
L000078	    \draw [thin] (0,1) -- (3,1);
L000079	    \draw [thin] (0,2) -- (3,2);
L000080	    \draw [thin] (1,0) -- (1,3);
L000081	    \draw [thin] (2,0) -- (2,3);
L000082	    \node at (1.5,-0.5) {1st iteration} ;
L000083	\end{scope}
L000084	
L000085	\begin{scope}[shift={(10,0)}]
L000086	    \draw (0,0) rectangle (3,3);
L000087	
L000088	    \draw [fill=black] (1/3,1/3) rectangle (2/3,2/3) ;
L000089	    \draw [fill=black] (1+1/3,1/3) rectangle (1+2/3,2/3) ;
L000090	    \draw [fill=black] (2+1/3,1/3) rectangle (2+2/3,2/3) ;
L000091	    \draw [fill=black] (1/3,1+1/3) rectangle (2/3,1+2/3) ;
L000092	    \draw [fill=black] (1/3,2+1/3) rectangle (2/3,2+2/3) ;
L000093	    \draw [fill=black] (2+1/3,2+1/3) rectangle (2+2/3,2+2/3) ;
L000094	    \draw [fill=black] (2+1/3,1+1/3) rectangle (2+2/3,1+2/3) ;
L000095	    \draw [fill=black] (1+1/3,2+1/3) rectangle (1+2/3,2+2/3) ;
L000096	
L000097	    \draw [fill=black] (1,1) rectangle (2,2) ;
L000098	    \draw [thin] (0,1) -- (3,1);
L000099	    \draw [thin] (0,2) -- (3,2);
L000100	    \draw [thin] (1,0) -- (1,3);
L000101	    \draw [thin] (2,0) -- (2,3);
L000102	
L000103	    \draw [ultra thin] (0,1/3) -- (3,1/3);
L000104	    \draw [ultra thin] (0,2/3) -- (3,2/3);
L000105	    \draw [ultra thin] (1/3,0) -- (1/3,3);
L000106	    \draw [ultra thin] (2/3,0) -- (2/3,3);
L000107	
L000108	    \draw [ultra thin] (0,1+1/3) -- (3,1+1/3);
L000109	    \draw [ultra thin] (0,1+2/3) -- (3,1+2/3);
L000110	    \draw [ultra thin] (1+1/3,0) -- (1+1/3,3);
L000111	    \draw [ultra thin] (1+2/3,0) -- (1+2/3,3);
L000112	
L000113	    \draw [ultra thin] (0,2+1/3) -- (3,2+1/3);
L000114	    \draw [ultra thin] (0,2+2/3) -- (3,2+2/3);
L000115	    \draw [ultra thin] (2+1/3,0) -- (2+1/3,3);
L000116	    \draw [ultra thin] (2+2/3,0) -- (2+2/3,3);
L000117	
L000118	    \node at (1.5,-0.5) {2nd iteration} ;
L000119	\end{scope}
L000120	\end{tikzpicture}
L000121	
L000122	\caption{Constructing the Sierpinski Carpet. We divide an initial square in a grid of $3\times3$ squares. We remove the central one. Then we can repeat this for the remaining eight squares. The area after one iteration is $A=s^2-N_{\text{holes}}=3^2-1=8$. So the mathematical theoretical dimension is $d^{th}_H=\log(8)/\log(3)=1.89$.}
L000123	
L000124	\label{fig:dimension schema}
L000125	\end{figure}
L000126	
L000127	In a purely mathematical context, two different fractals can share exactly the same fractal dimension. This occurs, for example, when one chooses the same subdivision factor, $s$ and the same number of removed sites $N_{\text{holes}}$, but removes them in a different geometric arrangement at each iteration. One could follow the same construction procedure as in Fig.~\ref{fig:dimension schema}, but instead remove, say, the upper-left square rather than the central one; the resulting object would have a drastically different visual appearance (see Fig.~\ref{fig:disconnected_pattern} (left)), yet its nominal dimension $d^{th}_H$ would remain identical because the scaling rule encoded in $\log(A)/\log(s)$ is unchanged.
L000128	
L000129	However, it is crucial to understand that in our physical system the final spatial profile of the bosonic density does not necessarily inherit this mathematical invariance: distinct driving geometries can lead to patterns that exhibit different effective fractal dimensions. This arises because the pattern is subject to physical distortions—such as nonlinear interactions, finite-resolution effects, and the intrinsic dynamics of the driven–dissipative medium—that modify how the nominal fractal mask is mapped onto the actual stationary density distribution. As a result, two drivings with mathematically identical fractal dimensions may generate bosonic patterns with noticeably different scaling behavior and thus different measured fractal dimensions.
L000130	
L000131	
L000132	To quantify the fractal dimension of the considered bosonic patterns, we employed the box-counting method \cite{Theiler_EstimatingDimension1990}. The shape of each pattern is first extracted from a grayscale image (see, for example, Fig.~\ref{fig:patterns-driving+densities}) by applying a threshold above which points are considered part of the pattern \cite{Otsu_Threshold1979}. This thresholding procedure delineates the fractal structure within the image, effectively separating the foreground (pattern) from the background and yielding a binary representation. In this binary image, pixels above the threshold are marked as belonging to the pattern, while those below are discarded. This representation serves as the basis for further spatial analysis.
L000133	
L000134	The box-counting method can be seen an inverse approach with respect to the fractal construction presented before. First, one divides the obtained binary image into a grid of $s^2$ square boxes, each with edge length $b = L/s$, where $L$ is the linear size of the system. We count the number of boxes occupied by the underlying density pattern, thereby estimating the area $A$ (in units of $b^2$) of the fractal, which corresponds to the white region shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:patterns-driving+densities}. In principle, this area scales as $A \propto s^{d_H}$, where $d_H$ is the Hausdorff (fractal) dimension. The box-counting dimension is thus given by the slope of $\log A = f(\log s)$ obtained over different scales $s$. By performing a linear fit to this curve, we extract an estimate of $d_H$, and the corresponding uncertainty is reported in the main text as the error bar on the box-counting dimension.
L000135	The patterns in Fig.~\ref{fig:patterns-driving+densities} are annotated with their respective box-counting dimensions $d_H$, which differ slightly from the theoretical nominal values $d^{th}_H$ for \textit{perfect}, infinitely self-similar fractals. For example, for the Sierpinski carpet (second column), $d^{th}_H = 1.89$, and for the Sierpinski cross (third column), $d^{th}_H = 1.46$. Nevertheless, even for these distorted and finite structures, we can determine a fractal dimension lying between 1 and~2.
L000136	
L000137	For patterns with fractal dimension equal to or lower than 1 (Fig.~\ref{fig:patterns-driving+densities}, last column), we used a slightly modified version of the box-counting method. We restricted the analysis to the horizontal dimension by fixing the vertical box size to the system size $L$, while the horizontal box length still scales as $b = L/s$. Instead of square boxes of size $b^2$, we thus used rectangular boxes of size $bL$. This allows for a consistent evaluation of fractal dimensions ranging from 0 to~2. For integer-dimensional patterns (1D and 2D), we did not apply the box-counting method, as their Hausdorff dimensions are trivially $d_H = 1$ and $d_H = 2$, respectively. Nonetheless, one could apply box-counting to the patterns shown in the first and fourth columns of Fig.~\ref{fig:patterns-driving+densities}; such an analysis would yield values close to 1 or 2, but never exactly. Indeed, precisely capturing integer dimensions via box-counting is challenging due to inherent estimation errors.
L000138	
L000139	At this point, we want to remind the reader explicitly that the value of the Hausdorff dimension calculated according to the equation $d_H^{\mathrm{th}} = \log(s^2 - N_{holes})/\log(s)$ and the value obtained by box-counting may differ from each other. Nevertheless, we emphasize that the fractal box-counting dimension $d_H$ is one of the key ingredients in characterizing the critical behavior of our system. For our conclusions, it is not essential whether two driving schemes share the same theoretical dimension $d_H^{\mathrm{th}}$; what matters is the Hausdorff dimension of the resulting bosonic density pattern, which is determined via box counting. In the main text, we focus on symmetric driving geometries for clarity. We expect that fractals with asymmetric driven regions will also exhibit behavior governed by their fractal dimension. To illustrate this point, we consider an asymmetric pattern constructed following the same iterative procedure as for the Sierpinski carpet, but with the upper-left square removed instead of the central one. The nominal fractal dimension is therefore identical to that of the Sierpinski carpet, since in both cases $s=3$ and $N_{\mathrm{holes}}=1$, yielding $d_H^{\mathrm{th}}=\log(9-1)/\log(3)=1.89$. However, the resulting fractal (see Fig.~\ref{fig:disconnected_pattern} (left)) is no longer symmetric with respect to the central vertical axis.
L000140	
L000141	\begin{figure}
L000142	    \centering
L000143	    \includegraphics[width=0.35\linewidth]{figures_SM/density_pattern_alternative.pdf}
L000144	    \includegraphics[width=0.5\linewidth]{figures_SM/ADR-vs-dH+disconnected.pdf}
L000145	    \caption{Left: The spatial appearance of a pattern with asymmetric driving. Its construction is the same as in Fig.~\ref{fig:dimension schema}, except now the upper left square is removed. Right: An edited version of Fig.~\ref{fig:ADR-vs-dH} of the main text where the assymetric patterns (the one shown on the left) is brought to the fore with a distinct colour (purple).}
L000146	    \label{fig:disconnected_pattern}
L000147	\end{figure}
L000148	
L000149	We can test that pattern in the same way we did in the main text. We obtain the result presented on the right-hand side of Fig.~\ref{fig:disconnected_pattern}. The resulting box-counting Hausdorff dimensions $d_H$ predicts the critical dynamical behavior. For the asymmetric pattern (purple dot), we have very similar results to the Sierpinski carpet (blue empty square), which was to be expected since their nominal dimensions $d^{th}_H$ are the same.
L000150	
L000151	\section{The spatial profiles of the studied fractals}
L000152	\begin{figure}[h]
L000153	    \centering
L000154	    \includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{figures_SM/patterns_drive+density_SM.pdf}
L000155	    \caption{Left panels: 2D square geometry ($d_H = 2$) and several variations of the Sierpinski carpet with fractal dimensions in the range $1 < d_H < 2$. Right panels: Variations of the Sierpinski cross ($1 < d_H < 2$) in the first two rows, followed by the 1D strip ($d_H = 1$) in the third row. The final row shows variations of the Cantor set with $d_H < 1$. All patterns are computed with $\Delta = \gamma$, and the driving amplitudes are tuned to their respective critical points. The geometries and system sizes correspond to those used in Fig.~\ref{fig:ADR-vs-dH}.}
L000156	\label{fig:other_patterns}
L000157	\end{figure}
L000158	Figure~\ref{fig:other_patterns} shows the spatial profiles of the patterns analyzed in the main text (Fig.~\ref{fig:ADR-vs-dH}). In addition to the standard 2D and 1D reference patterns, we include several variations of the Sierpinski cross and carpet, as well as modified versions of the Cantor set with fractal dimension lower than one.
L000159	
L000160	\iffalse
L000161	\newpage
L000162	
L000163	\section{Statistical mean field approach}
L000164	
L000165	The dissipative phase transition is related to the bistable behavior of the bosonic system, which is predicted by a single-mode mean-field approximation. The Gross-Pitaevskii equation~(\ref{eq:GPE}) in the steady state and for a homogeneous 2D driving becomes
L000166	\begin{equation}
L000167	    0=\left[-\Delta+g\left|\psi\right|^2-i\frac{\gamma}{2}\right]\psi
L000168	        +F \,.
L000169	\end{equation}
L000170	By isolating the driving on the left-hand side and by taking the modulus squared, we get, with $n=|\psi|^2$,
L000171	\begin{equation}
L000172	
L000173	        I=|F|^2=\left[(gn-\Delta)^2-\frac{\gamma^2}{4}\right]n\,.
L000174	
L000175	    \label{eq:bistability}
L000176	\end{equation}
L000177	This nonlinear relation gives the possible solutions of the density $n$ for a given driving intensity $I$, and can be called an equation of state~\cite{RMP2013}. For $\Delta>\sqrt{3}/2~\gamma$, this equation predicts a bistable behaviour, i.e., in a range of driving intensities, two stable solutions for the system's density $n$ exist~\cite{drummond_quantum_1980} (see Fig.~\ref{fig:mf-bistability}). This is a necessary condition for the appearance of the phase transition we are investigating. In this simple two-dimensional case, our mean-field approach predicts a critical detuning $\Delta_c^{MF}(d_H=2)=\sqrt{3}/2~\gamma$.
L000178	\begin{figure}[h]
L000179	    \centering
L000180	    \includegraphics[width=0.6\linewidth]{figures_SM/MFBistability.pdf}
L000181	    \caption{The mean-field equation of state (\ref{eq:bistability}) for different values of detuning $\Delta$. For $\Delta>\sqrt{3}/2~\gamma$, we see the typical S-shape of a bistability curve.}
L000182	    \label{fig:mf-bistability}
L000183	\end{figure}
L000184	
L000185	To account for dimensionality and extend this kind of analysis to fractal systems, one can take what we call a statistical mean field approach. First, we write the functional
L000186	
L000187	\[
L000188	\frac{1}{\hbar} \mathcal{F}[\psi]
L000189	=
L000190	\int \! dx\,dy \,
L000191	\left[
L000192	- \Delta |\psi|^2
L000193	+ \frac{\hbar}{2m} \, |\nabla \psi|^2
L000194	 + \frac{g}{2} |\psi|^4
L000195	- i \frac{\gamma}{2} |\psi|^2
L000196	+ F \psi^*
L000197	\right]\;,
L000198	\]
L000199	whose stationarization gives the Gross-Pitaevskii equation
L000200	\[
L000201	\frac{1}{\hbar}\,\frac{\delta \mathcal{F}}{\delta \psi^*} = 0
L000202	= - \Delta \psi -\frac{\hbar}{2m} \nabla^2 \psi
L000203	- i \frac{\gamma}{2} \psi
L000204	 + g |\psi|^2 \psi
L000205	+ F ~.
L000206	\]
L000207	
L000208	Our goal is to approximate the full functional \(\mathcal{F}[\psi]\) by a coarse-grained functional \(f(\phi)\), that keeps into account the driving pattern we use. Concretely, we partition space into square patches of size \(l\times l\), where \(l\) is the smallest characteristic length scale of the fractal pattern, and write the functional as
L000209	$$
L000210	\frac{1}{\hbar} \mathcal{F}[\psi]
L000211	=
L000212	\sum_p\int_0^l dx_p \int_0^l dy_p
L000213	\left[
L000214	\frac{\hbar}{2m} \, |\nabla\psi|^2
L000215	 + \frac{g}{2} |\psi|^4
L000216	- \Delta |\psi|^2 - i \frac{\gamma}{2} |\psi|^2
L000217	+ F\psi^*
L000218	\right],
L000219	$$
L000220	where the sum runs over the patches, and the subscript $p$ indicates the quantities within each patch.
L000221	
L000222	We assume the field to be zero in the non-driven patches ($F(x_p,y_p)=0$) and constant $\psi(x_p,y_p)\simeq \phi$ in the driven ones ($F(x_p,y_p)=F$). The kinetic term requires special care, as it is nonzero only at the interfaces between driven and undriven areas where the field is non-uniform. We can obtain an analytical description of this kinetic contribution by calculating the energy cost of such an interface. This means solving
L000223	\[
L000224	\frac{\hbar}{2m}\partial_x^2\psi(x)=-\left(\Delta+i\frac{\gamma}{2}\right)\psi(x) +F(x)
L000225	\]
L000226	for a step function of the driving field $F(x)=\theta(-x)F$. The linear solution ($g\to 0$) gives us a good estimate, because the density will be lower than in the bulk. The solution is a sum of the constant bulk solution $\phi$, in the driven region, and a plane wave amplitude $\frac{\phi}{2}$ and momentum
L000227	$$
L000228	k^2=\frac{2m}{\hbar}\left(\Delta+i\frac{\gamma}{2}\right).
L000229	$$
L000230	By approximating all interfaces to behave the same way, the kinetic gradient term can hence be substituted with $|\nabla\psi|^2\simeq |k|^2|\frac{\phi}{2}|^2$. Counting the number of edges for every patch $p$ then yields a contribution proportional to \((z-z_p)\), where \(z=4\) is the coordination number of the underlying 2D space and \( z_p\) is the local coordination number (i.e. the number of neighboring driven patches). The functional can hence be approximated by the following sum over driven patches:
L000231	\[
L000232	\frac{1}{\hbar} \mathcal{F}[\psi]
L000233	\approx
L000234	\sum_p l^2
L000235	\left[
L000236	- \Delta |\phi|^2
L000237	+ (z-z_p)\frac{\hbar}{8m} \, |k|^2|\phi|^2
L000238	 + \frac{g}{2} |\phi|^4
L000239	- i \frac{\gamma}{2} |\phi|^2
L000240	+ F \phi^*
L000241	\right]
L000242	\]
L000243	
L000244	We can now consider a rescaled and spatially averaged functional by introducing the average coordination number $\tilde{z}$ of the specific pattern:
L000245	\[
L000246	f(\phi)
L000247	=
L000248	- \Delta |\phi|^2
L000249	+(z-\tilde{z})\frac{\hbar}{8m} |k|^2 |\phi|^2
L000250	- i \frac{\gamma}{2} |\phi|^2
L000251	+ \frac{g}{2} |\phi|^4
L000252	+ F \phi^*~.
L000253	\]
L000254	Extremizing this averaged functional with respect to \(\phi^*\) yields the \textit{statistical} mean field steady-state equation
L000255	\begin{equation}
L000256	    \dot\phi=0=\left[-\Delta+(z-\tilde{z})\eta(\Delta)+g|\phi|^2-i\frac{\gamma}{2}\right]\phi+F,
L000257	\end{equation}
L000258	where we introduced the interface energy scale $\eta$,
L000259	\begin{equation}
L000260	\eta(\Delta)=\frac{\hbar}{8m} |k|^2=\frac{1}{4}\sqrt{\Delta^2+\gamma^2/4}~.
L000261	\end{equation}
L000262	
L000263	From this we can derive the equation of state
L000264	\begin{equation}
L000265	    I=\left[ \left(g|\phi|^2 -\Delta+(z-\tilde{z})\eta\right)^2+\frac{\gamma^2}{4} \right]|\phi|^2.
L000266	    \label{eq:bistability bis}
L000267	\end{equation}
L000268	This extends the usual single-mode description for a uniform system by introducing an additional term $\left(z-\tilde{z}\right)J$ that keeps the contribution of edges into account. We can now link the average the connectivity $\tilde{z}$ to the fractal dimension with the relation $\tilde{z}=2d_H$, that holds for integer dimensional systems.
L000269	
L000270	Just as equation \eqref{eq:bistability}, this equation of state will display bistability when  ${\Delta_{eff}=\Delta-(4-2d_H)\eta(\Delta)}>\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\gamma$. Since the bistability is a necessary condition for the presence of a phase transition, this gives us a dimensionality-dependent estimate for the critical detuning, below which the phase transition cannot occur
L000271	\begin{equation}\label{eq:mf-crit-detuning}
L000272	\begin{split}
L000273	    \Delta_c^{MF}(d_H)=&
L000274	     \Delta_c^{MF}(2)+(z-\tilde{z})\eta(\Delta_c^{MF}(d_H))\\
L000275	     =&\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\gamma+(4-2d_H)\eta(\Delta_c^{MF}(d_H))\\
L000276	     =&\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\gamma+\frac{(4-2d_H)}{4}\sqrt{\left(\Delta_c^{MF}(d_H)\right)^2+\gamma^2/4}~.
L000277	\end{split}
L000278	\end{equation}
L000279	This dependence is plotted with a dashed line in the inset of figure~\ref{fig:ADR-vs-Delta}. One can see that the curve follows the same trend as the numerical data, remaining below them, as expected. Moreover, equation \eqref{eq:mf-crit-detuning} predicts that the critical detuning diverges when the dimensionality goes to zero, as was found for single-mode Kerr resonators.
