L000313	\section{Asymptotic decay rate and Bogoliubov instabilities}
L000314	
L000315	For a homogeneous driving, we can diagonalize the problem for fluctuations on top of the steady state and obtain a spectrum of the so-called dissipative Bogoliubov modes \cite{RMP2013}. By analyzing the dispersion relation given in equation~(\ref{eq:bogo}), we can interpret the phase transition as the emergence of an unstable mode.
L000316	
L000317	\begin{equation}
L000318	    \omega_{\mathrm{Bog}}(\mathbf{k})= \pm\left[\left(-\Delta+\frac{\hbar \mathbf{k}^2}{2 m}+2 gn\right)^2-\left(g n\right)^2\right]^{1 / 2} -i\frac{\gamma}{2}~.
L000319	\label{eq:bogo}
L000320	\end{equation}
L000321	
L000322	All modes decay with the natural polariton decay rate $\gamma$; however, this rate can be reduced and is determined by the imaginary part of $\omega_{\mathrm{Bog}}$. If $\Im\left(\omega_{\mathrm{Bog}}(\mathbf{k})\right) < 0$, the mode is stable and has a linewidth given by $2\Im\left(\omega_{\mathrm{Bog}}(\mathbf{k})\right)$. The factor of 2 arises because we are considering the evolution of the bosonic density, $n(t) = |\psi(t)|^2$.
L000323	
L000324	An unstable mode corresponds to $\Im\left(\omega_{\mathrm{Bog}}(\mathbf{k})\right) \geq 0$. In this case, the linewidth vanishes, the asymptotic decay rate tends to zero, and we can interpret the system as undergoing critical behavior.
L000325	
L000326	We can define the minimal linewidth $\lambda_{\mathrm{Bog}}$ associated with the temporal evolution of the density of a homogeneous phase with steady-state density $n_{ss}$ as:
L000327	\begin{equation}
L000328	\lambda_{\mathrm{Bog}}=
L000329	  \left\{
L000330	    \begin{array}{l}
L000331	      2|\max_{k,n_{ss}}\Im\left(\omega_{\mathrm{Bog}}(k,n_{ss})\right)| \text{ if stable}\\
L000332	      0 \text{ else  .}
L000333	    \end{array}
L000334	  \right.
L000335	\end{equation}
L000336	
L000337	After calculations, this leads to the following expression:
L000338	\begin{equation}
L000339	\lambda_{\mathrm{Bog}}=
L000340	  \left\{
L000341	    \begin{array}{l}
L000342	    \gamma  \text{   if }\Delta<0 \\
L000343	      \gamma-\frac{2}{\sqrt{3}}\Delta \text{   if }0<\Delta<\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\gamma \\
L000344	      0 \text{   else . }
L000345	    \end{array}
L000346	  \right.
L000347	\end{equation}
L000348	
L000349	This dependence is shown as the dotted line in Fig.~\ref{fig:bogo instabilities}, alongside numerical results for the 2D square geometry. Both the analytical predictions and the numerical data display a step-like behavior with a linear slope between the upper and lower values. The two curves coincide for $\Delta \gtrsim 1.0\gamma$, where the system becomes critical and $\lambda_{\min} = 0$. Deviations at other detuning values can be primarily attributed to finite-size effects.
L000350	
L000351	
L000352	
L000353	
L000354	\begin{figure}[h]
L000355	    \centering
L000356	    \includegraphics[width=0.6\linewidth]{figures_SM/ADR-vs-Delta_withBogo.pdf}
L000357	    \caption{Minimal asymptotic decay rate for the 2D square geometry (green squares), computed for a system size $L = 81l$. Vertical error bars indicate the difference in $\lambda_{\min}$ between this size and a smaller system with $L = 27l$. The grey dash-dotted line shows the linewidth $\lambda_{\mathrm{Bog}}$ predicted by Bogoliubov analysis for a uniform 2D system. The two curves exhibit similar behavior, with discrepancies attributed to finite-size effects.}
L000358	
L000359	    \label{fig:bogo instabilities}
L000360	\end{figure}
