L000612	{\it Statistical mean field approach ---}
L000613	The dissipative phase transition is related to the bistable behavior of the bosonic system, which is predicted within a single-mode mean-field approximation. In this framework, the Gross-Pitaevskii equation~(\ref{eq:GPE}) in the steady state and for a homogeneous 2D driving reduces to
L000614	\begin{equation}
L000615	    0=\left[-\Delta+g\left|\psi\right|^2-i\frac{\gamma}{2}\right]\psi
L000616	        +F \,.
L000617	\end{equation}
L000618	By isolating the driving term on the left-hand side and taking the modulus squared, one obtains, with $n=|\psi|^2$,
L000619	\begin{equation}
L000620	        I=|F|^2=\left[(gn-\Delta)^2+\frac{\gamma^2}{4}\right]n\,.
L000621	    \label{eq:bistability}
L000622	\end{equation}
L000623	This nonlinear relation determines the possible steady-state solutions for the density $n$ at a given driving intensity $I$ and can be interpreted as an equation of state~\cite{RMP2013}. For $\Delta>\sqrt{3}/2~\gamma$, this equation predicts a bistable behavior, namely the coexistence of two stable density solutions over a finite range of driving intensities~\cite{drummond_quantum_1980}. While this bistability does not correspond to a phase transition in a single-mode system, it constitutes a necessary condition for the appearance of the dissipative phase transition investigated here. In the homogeneous two-dimensional case, this mean-field analysis yields a critical detuning $\Delta_c^{MF}(d_H=2)=\sqrt{3}/2~\gamma$.
L000624	
L000625	To incorporate the role of dimensionality and extend this type of analysis to fractal systems, we introduce what we refer to as a statistical mean-field approach. We start by writing the functional
L000626	\begin{align*}
L000627	\frac{1}{\hbar} \mathcal{F}[\psi]
L000628	=\int \! dx\,dy \,
L000629	\bigg[
L000630	& - \Delta |\psi|^2
L000631	+ \frac{\hbar}{2m} \, |\nabla \psi|^2 \\
L000632	& + \frac{g}{2} |\psi|^4
L000633	- i \frac{\gamma}{2} |\psi|^2
L000634	+ F \left(\psi^*+\psi\right)
L000635	\bigg]\;,
L000636	\end{align*}
L000637	whose stationarization yields the Gross-Pitaevskii equation
L000638	\begin{align*}
L000639	\frac{1}{\hbar}\,\frac{\delta \mathcal{F}}{\delta \psi^*} &= 0\\
L000640	&= - \Delta \psi -\frac{\hbar}{2m} \nabla^2 \psi
L000641	- i \frac{\gamma}{2} \psi
L000642	 + g |\psi|^2 \psi
L000643	+ F ~.
L000644	\end{align*}
L000645	Our goal is to approximate the full functional $\mathcal{F}[\psi]$ with a coarse-graining that explicitly accounts for the spatial structure of the driving pattern. Concretely, we partition space into square patches of size $l\times l$, where $l$ is the smallest characteristic length scale of the fractal pattern, and write the functional as
L000646	\begin{align*}
L000647	\frac{1}{\hbar} \mathcal{F}[\psi]
L000648	=
L000649	\sum_p\int_0^l dx_p \int_0^l dy_p
L000650	\bigg[ &
L000651	- \Delta |\psi|^2 +
L000652	\frac{\hbar}{2m} \, |\nabla\psi|^2 \\
L000653	& + \frac{g}{2} |\psi|^4
L000654	- i \frac{\gamma}{2} |\psi|^2
L000655	+ F\psi^*
L000656	\bigg]~,
L000657	\end{align*}
L000658	where the sum runs over the patches and the subscript $p$ labels quantities within each patch.
L000659	
L000660	We assume the field to vanish in the non-driven patches ($F(x_p,y_p)=0$) and to be approximately constant, $\psi(x_p,y_p)\simeq \phi$, in the driven ones ($F(x_p,y_p)=F$). The kinetic term requires special care, as it contributes only at the interfaces between driven and undriven regions, where the field varies spatially. An analytical expression for this kinetic contribution can be obtained by computing the energy cost associated with such an interface neglecting $g \vert \Psi \vert^2$. This is a good approximation because the inclusion of this term only slightly distorts the linear solution for the density near the pump edge. This amounts to solving
L000661	\[
L000662	\frac{\hbar}{2m}\partial_x^2\psi(x)=-\left(\Delta+i\frac{\gamma}{2}\right)\psi(x) +F(x)
L000663	\]
L000664	for a step-like driving field $F(x)=\theta(-x)F$. The solution can be expressed as the sum of the constant bulk solution $\phi$ in the driven region and a plane-wave contribution with amplitude $\frac{\phi}{2}$ and momentum
L000665	$$
L000666	k^2=\frac{2m}{\hbar}\left(\Delta+i\frac{\gamma}{2}\right).
L000667	$$
L000668	By approximating all interfaces as behaving identically, the kinetic gradient term can thus be replaced by $|\nabla\psi|^2\simeq |k|^2|\frac{\phi}{2}|^2$. Counting the number of edges associated with each patch $p$ then yields a contribution proportional to $(z-z_p)$, where $z=4$ is the coordination number of the underlying two-dimensional lattice and $z_p$ is the local coordination number, defined as the number of neighboring driven patches. The functional can therefore be approximated by the following sum over driven patches:
L000669	\begin{align*}
L000670	    \frac{1}{\hbar} \mathcal{F}[\psi]
L000671	\approx
L000672	\sum_p l^2
L000673	\bigg[&
L000674	- \Delta |\phi|^2
L000675	+ (z-z_p)\frac{\hbar}{8m} \, |k|^2|\phi|^2\\
L000676	& + \frac{g}{2} |\phi|^4
L000677	- i \frac{\gamma}{2} |\phi|^2
L000678	+ F \phi^*
L000679	\bigg]~.
L000680	\end{align*}
L000681	
L000682	We can now introduce a rescaled and spatially averaged functional by defining the average coordination number $\tilde{z}$ associated with the specific driving pattern:
L000683	\[
L000684	f(\phi)
L000685	=
L000686	- \Delta |\phi|^2
L000687	+(z-\tilde{z})\frac{\hbar}{8m} |k|^2 |\phi|^2
L000688	- i \frac{\gamma}{2} |\phi|^2
L000689	+ \frac{g}{2} |\phi|^4
L000690	+ F \phi^*~.
L000691	\]
L000692	Extremizing this with respect to $\phi^*$ yields the \textit{statistical} mean-field steady-state equation
L000693	\begin{equation}
L000694	    \dot\phi=0=\left[-\Delta+(z-\tilde{z})\,\eta(\Delta)+g|\phi|^2-i\frac{\gamma}{2}\right]\phi+F,
L000695	\end{equation}
L000696	where we introduced the interface energy scale
L000697	\begin{equation}
L000698	\eta(\Delta)=\frac{\hbar}{8m} |k|^2=\frac{1}{4}\sqrt{\Delta^2+\gamma^2/4}~.
L000699	\end{equation}
L000700	From this expression, we can derive the corresponding equation of state
L000701	\begin{equation}
L000702	    I=\left[ \left(g|\phi|^2 -\Delta+(z-\tilde{z})\,\eta\right)^2+\frac{\gamma^2}{4} \right]|\phi|^2.
L000703	    \label{eq:bistability bis}
L000704	\end{equation}
L000705	This formulation extends the usual single-mode description of a uniform system by incorporating an additional term $(z-\tilde{z})\,\eta$ that accounts for the energetic cost of interfaces. We can further relate the average connectivity $\tilde{z}$ to the fractal dimension through the relation $\tilde{z}=2d_H$, which holds for integer-dimensional systems.
L000706	
L000707	As in Eq.~\eqref{eq:bistability}, this equation of state exhibits bistability when
L000708	\begin{equation}
L000709	    {\Delta_{eff}=\Delta-(4-2d_H)\,\eta(\Delta)}>\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\gamma~.
L000710	\end{equation}
L000711	Since bistability is a necessary condition for the existence of a phase transition, this relation provides a dimensionality-dependent estimate of the critical detuning below which the phase transition cannot occur,
L000712	\begin{equation}\label{eq:mf-crit-detuning}
L000713	\begin{split}
L000714	    \Delta_c^{MF}(d_H)=&
L000715	     \Delta_c^{MF}(2)+(z-\tilde{z}) \, \eta\big(\Delta_c^{MF}(d_H)\big)\\
L000716	     =&\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\gamma+(4-2d_H)\,\eta\big(\Delta_c^{MF}(d_H)\big)\\
L000717	     =&\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\gamma+\frac{(4-2d_H)}{4}\sqrt{\big(\Delta_c^{MF}(d_H)\big)^2+\frac{\gamma^2}{4}}~.
L000718	\end{split}
L000719	\end{equation}
L000720	This dependence is shown as a dashed line in the inset of Fig.~\ref{fig:ADR-vs-Delta}. The resulting curve follows the same qualitative trend as the numerical data while remaining below them, as expected for a lower bound. Moreover, Eq.~\eqref{eq:mf-crit-detuning} predicts that the critical detuning diverges as the dimensionality approaches zero, in agreement with the behavior found in single-mode Kerr resonators \cite{Rodriguez2017}.
